    "result_text": "Done. The change is implemented and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe app derives read-only owner attributes in `OwnerDerivations`, exposes them as `@Transient` getters on `Owner`, and MapStruct maps them onto the `OwnerDto` generated from `openapi.yml`. I followed that same pattern to add `timezone`:\n\n1. **`OwnerDerivations.java`** \u2014 added a fixed `REGION_TIMEZONE` table (`NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney`, `VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne`, `QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane`) and a `timezone(String locality)` method that looks up the IANA name, returning `null` for unknown regions (e.g. `\"UNKNOWN\"`).\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `@Transient getTimezone()` that feeds the owner's derived `getLocality()` (its canonical region) into the new derivation.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `timezone` string property to the owner schema (right after `locality`), so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field and MapStruct populates it automatically.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreReturnsTimezoneForRegion` now sees `$.locality == \"NSW\"` and `$.timezone == \"Australia/Sydney\"` for a Sydney/postcode-2000 owner, and it passes along with the rest of the suite.\n\nSince `timezone` is derived from the *locality* rather than the raw city, it also correctly reflects the postcode-based region disambiguation the locality already applies.",
